\section{Expose Parallelism Through Values}

Val and its descendant SISAL use single-assignment, side-effect-free values to expose independent work. Arrays are values rather than collections of independently mutable cells. A parallel loop can construct array elements or reduce contributions without alias races; a recurrence loop instead creates new loop-parameter bindings from the previous iteration. SISAL extended this family with recursion, streams, and richer loop forms \cite{feo1990sisal}.

Parallel syntax does not promise unlimited simultaneous hardware. It states independence and a result-combination rule; a compiler may chunk, vectorize, distribute, or sequentialize iterations. Reduction order needs particular care. Associativity in mathematical reals does not make machine floating-point addition associative.

\section{Construct and Reduce in a Val \texttt{forall}}

This Val-style loop returns a tuple containing a sum and an array:

\begin{verbatim}
forall i in [1, 100] do
  area: real := width[i] * height[i];
  okay: Boolean := acceptable(area);
  accumulate + if okay then area else 0.0 end;
  construct if okay then area else 0.0 end;
end
\end{verbatim}

Each iteration binds its own \texttt{i}, \texttt{area}, and \texttt{okay}. \texttt{construct} fixes array position by iteration identity; \texttt{accumulate +} declares a combining operation. A SISAL compiler can represent similar loops as parallel graph regions and apply array storage optimizations while preserving value semantics. \Cref{fig:visual-366} separates independent production from the merge.

