% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Lower a Parallel Loop Region}

\begin{verbatim}
region := create_parallel_region(index_range)
for each body binding:
    create typed single-assignment node in region
array_result := gather_by_index(region.contributions)
reduction_result := reduce(region.terms, declared_operator,
                           required_numeric_order)
analyze_array_uniqueness_and_last_use()
choose sequential, vector, threaded, or distributed schedule
\end{verbatim}

The chosen schedule is not embedded in the source value graph. If the language fixes only an associative reduction and the operator truly satisfies that contract, a tree is legal; otherwise the IR must retain a required order.

\section{Reuse Arrays Without Exposing Mutation}

The IR should contain operation, loop-region, gather, and reduction nodes; value, index, and recurrence edges; explicit loop-carried state; iteration domains and any rates; array shapes and element types; numeric and I/O effects; and provenance from generated nodes to loop clauses. Compilation can produce vector loops, fork--join tasks, distributed kernels, or ordinary machine code with preallocated arrays.

Legal optimizations include fusion, loop interchange, tiling, vectorization, and in-place array reuse when dependence, bounds, exception behavior, and uniqueness proofs permit them. Semantic-preservation risks include racing two contributions to one index, retaining an alias to overwritten storage, changing which error is observed, and reassociating floating-point sums. Preserve returned arrays elementwise and reductions under the language's numeric contract; physical mutation is acceptable only while observationally implementing immutable values.

\section{Give the Gather and the Reduction Different Orders}

Use a four-iteration slice of the loop and accept only nonnegative areas. For widths \([2,-1,4,1]\) and heights \([3,5,2,1]\), the contribution array is \([6,0,8,1]\) and the total is \(15\). A worker may finish iteration 3 first without moving its value to array slot 1. Waiting for all contributions and indexing the gather by source iteration makes that distinction explicit.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{crrr}
\hline
Iteration & Area & Contribution & Arrival position in one run \\
\hline
1 & 6 & 6 & 3 \\
2 & -5 & 0 & 2 \\
3 & 8 & 8 & 1 \\
4 & 1 & 1 & 4 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
parallel for i in legal_index_range:
    value := contribution(width[i], height[i])
    results[i] := value
join_all_workers()
total := identity
for i in legal_index_range_in_source_order:
    total := numeric_add(total, results[i])
return (freeze(results), total)
\end{verbatim}

Even if a source reduction requires left-to-right floating-point addition, the contributions can be computed in parallel when the computations are pure and total. A tree reduction is a separate transformation. For example, on typical binary floating-point numbers \((10^{20}+(-10^{20}))+1\) retains \(1\), while \(10^{20}+((-10^{20})+1)\) yields zero. If reassociation is permitted, a balanced tree can reduce span; otherwise keep the specified reduction order. Include a zero-length range in the tests if the source permits it. The gather must be empty and the reduction must follow the specified empty-case rule; an implementation must not read the first array element to obtain a seed when there are none.

\paragraph{Prove ownership of destination slices.}
For a contiguous index range and ordinary element storage, partition
the result into disjoint index intervals and allow each worker to write
only its interval. That proof does not carry over to arbitrary views.
If two logical indices map to one byte range, or two Boolean elements
are packed into the same machine word, logical disjointness may not
give independent physical writes. Lower packed construction by staging
private results and packing afterward, or partition by whole storage
words. For the four-iteration fixture, check the result both with one
worker and with one worker per contribution; each logical index must
be initialized exactly once before the array becomes visible.
